{% extends "base.peb" %}
{% block title %}Deployment{% endblock %}
{% block content %}
    <h1>Deploy the API to EC2</h1>
    <p class="lead">The API runs on an AWS EC2 instance, with PostgreSQL on the same machine. The instance serves only
        the backend. The website is hosted on GitHub Pages and calls the API over HTTPS.</p>

    <table>
        <thead>
        <tr>
            <th>Setting</th>
            <th>Value</th>
        </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td>Elastic IP</td>
            <td><code>44.254.208.38</code></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td>Public DNS name</td>
            <td><code>ec2-44-254-208-38.us-west-2.compute.amazonaws.com</code></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td>API URL</td>
            <td><code>https://44.254.208.38</code>, or <code>https://api.findwic.com</code> once its DNS record is set
                up
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td>Database user</td>
            <td><code>app</code></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td>Database name</td>
            <td><code>app_</code> followed by four hex characters, such as <code>app_1a2b</code>. These pages write it
                as <code>app_xxxx</code>.
            </td>
        </tr>
        </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>The commands below assume Amazon Linux 2023, the <code>ec2-user</code> login user, and the repository cloned to
        <code>/home/ec2-user/api-wicit-doc</code>. Change the paths if yours differ.</p>

    <h2>How requests reach the API</h2>
    <p>The website is served over HTTPS, and browsers block its requests to a plain HTTP API. So
        <a href="https://caddyserver.com/">Caddy</a> listens on ports 80 and 443, gets a free certificate from Let's
        Encrypt, and forwards requests to the API on <code>127.0.0.1:8080</code>. The API and PostgreSQL only listen on
        the instance itself.</p>
    <pre><code>Browser (GitHub Pages site)
  → https://44.254.208.38      Caddy, ports 80 and 443
  → http://127.0.0.1:8080      Spring Boot API
  → localhost:5432             PostgreSQL</code></pre>
    <p class="note">Let's Encrypt does not issue certificates for <code>*.amazonaws.com</code> names, so the API uses
        the Elastic IP address instead. Certificates for an IP address last about six days. Caddy renews them
        automatically. To use <code>api.findwic.com</code> instead, see
        <a href="#use-api-findwic-com">Use api.findwic.com</a>.</p>

    <h2>1. Open the ports</h2>
    <p>In the EC2 console, edit the instance's security group so its inbound rules are:</p>
    <table>
        <thead>
        <tr>
            <th>Port</th>
            <th>Source</th>
            <th>Why</th>
        </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td>22 (SSH)</td>
            <td>Your IP only</td>
            <td>Administration.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td>80 (HTTP)</td>
            <td><code>0.0.0.0/0</code></td>
            <td>Let's Encrypt checks this port before it issues a certificate. Caddy redirects everything else to
                HTTPS.
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td>443 (HTTPS)</td>
            <td><code>0.0.0.0/0</code></td>
            <td>The API.</td>
        </tr>
        </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>Do not open 8080 or 5432.</p>

    <h2>2. Add swap</h2>
    <p>A <code>t2.micro</code> has 1 GB of memory for the API, PostgreSQL and Caddy together. Add 2 GB of swap so a
        busy moment slows the instance down instead of stopping a process:</p>
    <pre><code>sudo dd if=/dev/zero of=/swapfile bs=1M count=2048
sudo chmod 600 /swapfile
sudo mkswap /swapfile
sudo swapon /swapfile
echo '/swapfile none swap defaults 0 0' | sudo tee -a /etc/fstab</code></pre>

    <h2 id="install-jdk">3. Install JDK 27</h2>
    <p>Install Amazon Corretto 27 from its tarball into <code>/opt/java</code>. The link
        <code>/opt/java/corretto-27</code> points to the installed version, so the service file and
        <code>PATH</code> don't change when you upgrade:</p>
    <pre><code>sudo dnf install -y wget
cd /tmp
wget https://corretto.aws/downloads/latest/amazon-corretto-27-x64-linux-jdk.tar.gz
sudo mkdir -p /opt/java
sudo tar -xzf amazon-corretto-27-x64-linux-jdk.tar.gz -C /opt/java
ls /opt/java</code></pre>
    <p><code>ls</code> shows the folder the tarball created, such as <code>amazon-corretto-27.0.1.8.1-linux-x64</code>.
        The version in the name changes with each release. Link the folder, check the link works, and put Java on
        everyone's <code>PATH</code>:</p>
    <pre><code>sudo ln -sfn "$(ls -d /opt/java/amazon-corretto-27* | sort -V | tail -1)" /opt/java/corretto-27
/opt/java/corretto-27/bin/java -version
echo 'export JAVA_HOME=/opt/java/corretto-27
export PATH="$JAVA_HOME/bin:$PATH"' | sudo tee /etc/profile.d/java.sh
source /etc/profile.d/java.sh
java -version</code></pre>
    <p>The API runs on this JDK. Gradle never runs on the instance. To upgrade later, extract the new
        tarball, point the link at the new folder, and restart the API.</p>

    <h2>4. Set up PostgreSQL</h2>
    <p>Install PostgreSQL 18, create its data folder, and start it at boot:</p>
    <pre><code>sudo dnf install -y postgresql18 postgresql18-server postgresql18-contrib
sudo postgresql-setup --initdb
sudo systemctl enable --now postgresql</code></pre>
    <p>Create the <code>app</code> user and the database. Pick four hex characters for the name, and a strong
        password:</p>
    <pre><code>sudo -u postgres psql -c "create user app with password 'your-password';"
sudo -u postgres psql -c "create database app_xxxx owner app;"</code></pre>
    <p>By default, PostgreSQL on Amazon Linux checks local network connections with <code>ident</code>, which rejects
        passwords. The API connects over <code>localhost</code> with a password, so change those lines. Open the file
        with <code>sudo nano /var/lib/pgsql/data/pg_hba.conf</code> and change the <code>host</code> lines for
        <code>127.0.0.1/32</code> and <code>::1/128</code> to use <code>scram-sha-256</code>:</p>
    <pre><code>host    all    all    127.0.0.1/32    scram-sha-256
host    all    all    ::1/128         scram-sha-256</code></pre>
    <p>Then restart PostgreSQL and check that the <code>app</code> user can connect. Put your password and database
        name in place of <code>PASSWORD</code> and <code>app_xxxx</code>:</p>
    <pre><code>sudo systemctl restart postgresql
psql "postgresql://app:PASSWORD@localhost:5432/app_xxxx" -c 'select version();'</code></pre>
    <p>PostgreSQL only listens on <code>localhost</code> unless you change <code>listen_addresses</code>, so leave that
        setting alone. If the password contains characters that have a meaning in URLs, such as <code>@</code>,
        <code>/</code> or <code>:</code>, percent-encode them in this command. In the <code>.env</code> file below,
        write the password as it is.</p>

    <h2>5. Set the environment variables</h2>
    <p>The API reads one file, <code>api-wicit-java/.env</code>, whether you start it by hand or as a service. Git
        ignores it, so it is never
        committed. Create it:</p>
    <pre><code>cd ~/api-wicit-doc/api-wicit-java
nano .env</code></pre>
    <p>Write each value on one line, without quotes:</p>
    <pre><code>SPRING_PROFILES_ACTIVE=prod
DB_URL=jdbc:postgresql://localhost:5432/app_xxxx
DB_USERNAME=app
DB_PASSWORD=your-password
SERVER_ADDRESS=127.0.0.1
PORT=8080</code></pre>
    <p>Then make it readable only by you:</p>
    <pre><code>chmod 600 .env</code></pre>
    <table>
        <thead>
        <tr>
            <th>Variable</th>
            <th>Value on EC2</th>
        </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td><code>SPRING_PROFILES_ACTIVE</code></td>
            <td><code>prod</code>. Requires every database setting and hides health details.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><code>DB_URL</code></td>
            <td>The JDBC URL. It has no user or password, unlike the <code>psql</code> URL.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><code>DB_USERNAME</code></td>
            <td><code>app</code></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><code>DB_PASSWORD</code></td>
            <td>The <code>app</code> user's password.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><code>SERVER_ADDRESS</code></td>
            <td><code>127.0.0.1</code>, so the API only accepts connections from Caddy on the same machine.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><code>PORT</code></td>
            <td><code>8080</code>, the port Caddy forwards to.</td>
        </tr>
        </tbody>
    </table>

    <h2>6. Connect from your computer</h2>
    <p>A <code>t2.micro</code> can't run Gradle: building the API or loading the data on it runs out of memory and
        freezes the instance. So you build and seed on your own computer, then upload the results. The instance only
        runs Java, PostgreSQL and Caddy.</p>
    <p>Each step below says whether its commands run in PowerShell on your computer or on the server over SSH. In
        PowerShell, set the key and server once per window:</p>
    <pre><code>$Key = "C:\Users\nateb\Downloads\Wicit_Secret_Key2.pem"
$Server = "ec2-user@44.254.208.38"</code></pre>
    <p>SSH refuses a key file that other users can read. Remove the inherited permissions so only you can read it.
        This only needs doing once:</p>
    <pre><code>icacls $Key /inheritance:r /grant:r "${env:USERNAME}:R"</code></pre>
    <p>Check the connection:</p>
    <pre><code>ssh -i $Key $Server "echo connected"</code></pre>

    <h2 id="load-the-data">7. Load the data</h2>
    <p>Seed your local database, copy it to a dump file, and restore that file on the server. The dump includes
        Liquibase's own tables, so both databases agree on which changesets have run.</p>
    <p>On your computer, bring the local database up to date and dump it. It uses the local settings in
        <code>api-wicit-java/.env</code>, such as <code>app_28c3</code> and the <code>nateb</code> user:</p>
    <pre><code>cd C:\Users\nateb\Source\Repos\api-wicit-doc\api-wicit-java
.\gradlew.bat update
pg_dump -h localhost -U nateb -d app_28c3 --format=custom --no-owner --no-privileges --file "$env:TEMP\wicit.dump"
scp -i $Key "$env:TEMP\wicit.dump" "${Server}:/tmp/wicit.dump"</code></pre>
    <p>On the server, restore it into the <code>app</code> database. <code>--clean</code> replaces any tables that are
        already there, and <code>--single-transaction</code> leaves the database unchanged if anything fails:</p>
    <p>Stop the API first if it is already running, whichever way you started it. Each command does nothing if the API
        isn't running that way:</p>
    <pre><code>sudo systemctl stop wicit-api 2&gt;/dev/null      # the service
pkill -f '[w]icit-api-0.0.1-SNAPSHOT.jar'           # a manual run</code></pre>
    <p>Then restore:</p>
    <pre><code>pg_restore -h localhost -U app -d app_xxxx --no-owner --no-privileges --clean --if-exists --single-transaction /tmp/wicit.dump
rm /tmp/wicit.dump</code></pre>
    <p>Then check the row counts:</p>
    <pre><code>psql "postgresql://app:PASSWORD@localhost:5432/app_xxxx" -c "
select 'vendor' as table_name, count(*) from vendor
union all select 'authorized_product', count(*) from authorized_product
union all select 'farmers_market', count(*) from farmers_market
union all select 'wic_farmer', count(*) from wic_farmer
union all select 'participants_by_category', count(*) from participants_by_category
union all select 'participants_by_race_ethnicity', count(*) from participants_by_race_ethnicity
union all select 'redemption_by_family', count(*) from redemption_by_family
union all select 'redemption_by_participant_category', count(*) from redemption_by_participant_category;"</code></pre>
    <p>Every table should have rows, with the same counts as your local database.</p>

    <h2 id="build-and-upload">8. Build and upload the API</h2>
    <p>On your computer, build the jar and copy it to the server. It uploads under a temporary name and is then moved
        into place, so a running API never reads a half-copied file:</p>
    <pre><code>cd C:\Users\nateb\Source\Repos\api-wicit-doc\api-wicit-java
.\gradlew.bat bootJar
ssh -i $Key $Server "mkdir -p ~/api-wicit-doc/api-wicit-java/build/libs"
scp -i $Key build\libs\wicit-api-0.0.1-SNAPSHOT.jar "${Server}:api-wicit-doc/api-wicit-java/build/libs/wicit-api.jar.new"
ssh -i $Key $Server "mv ~/api-wicit-doc/api-wicit-java/build/libs/wicit-api.jar.new ~/api-wicit-doc/api-wicit-java/build/libs/wicit-api-0.0.1-SNAPSHOT.jar"</code></pre>
    <p>A jar built on Windows runs on Linux unchanged. It includes the
        dataset files and doesn't need a database to build.</p>

    <h2 id="run-the-api">9. Run the API</h2>
    <p>There are two ways to run the API. Use one at a time: both listen on port 8080, so stop one before starting the
        other.</p>
    <table>
        <thead>
        <tr>
            <th></th>
            <th>Manual</th>
            <th>systemd service</th>
        </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td>Survives logging out of SSH</td>
            <td>Yes, with <code>nohup</code></td>
            <td>Yes</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td>Survives a reboot</td>
            <td>No. Start it again by hand.</td>
            <td>Yes. It starts at boot.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td>Restarts if it crashes</td>
            <td>No</td>
            <td>Yes</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td>Logs</td>
            <td><code>~/wicit-api.log</code></td>
            <td><code>journalctl -u wicit-api</code></td>
        </tr>
        </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>In both, <code>-Xmx384m</code> caps the heap so the API, PostgreSQL and Caddy fit in 1 GB.</p>

    <h3 id="run-manually">Option 1: Run it manually</h3>
    <p>Start the API in the background from <code>api-wicit-java</code>. The API reads <code>.env</code> from the
        folder it starts in, so run it from there:</p>
    <pre><code>cd ~/api-wicit-doc/api-wicit-java
nohup java -Xmx384m -jar build/libs/wicit-api-0.0.1-SNAPSHOT.jar &gt; ~/wicit-api.log 2&gt;&amp;1 &amp;</code></pre>
    <p><code>nohup</code> keeps it running after you log out of SSH. It stops when the instance reboots. Check that it
        started with the <code>prod</code> profile and is healthy:</p>
    <pre><code>grep 'profile is active' ~/wicit-api.log
curl http://127.0.0.1:8080/actuator/health</code></pre>
    <p>The log should say <code>The following 1 profile is active: "prod"</code>, and the health check returns
        <code>{"status":"UP"}</code> once the API has connected to the database. It can take half a minute to start.
        To follow the log, run <code>tail -f ~/wicit-api.log</code>.</p>
    <p>To check whether it is running, and to stop it:</p>
    <pre><code>pgrep -af wicit-api
pkill -f '[w]icit-api-0.0.1-SNAPSHOT.jar'</code></pre>
    <p>To run it in the foreground instead, for example while you debug, leave out <code>nohup</code>, the redirect and
        the <code>&amp;</code>. The log then prints to the terminal, and <kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>C</kbd> stops it. It
        also stops when you log out.</p>

    <h3 id="run-as-a-service">Option 2: Run it as a systemd service</h3>
    <p>systemd starts the API at boot and restarts it if it stops. If the API is running manually, stop it first with
        <code>pkill -f '[w]icit-api-0.0.1-SNAPSHOT.jar'</code>. Then create the service file. Copy the whole command,
        through the final <code>EOF</code>. It replaces the file, so running it again is safe:</p>
    <pre><code>sudo tee /etc/systemd/system/wicit-api.service &gt; /dev/null &lt;&lt;'EOF'
[Unit]
Description=WICit API
After=network-online.target postgresql.service
Wants=network-online.target

[Service]
User=ec2-user
WorkingDirectory=/home/ec2-user/api-wicit-doc/api-wicit-java
EnvironmentFile=/home/ec2-user/api-wicit-doc/api-wicit-java/.env
ExecStart=/opt/java/corretto-27/bin/java -Xmx384m -jar build/libs/wicit-api-0.0.1-SNAPSHOT.jar
SuccessExitStatus=143
Restart=on-failure

[Install]
WantedBy=multi-user.target
EOF</code></pre>
    <p>Start the service, set it to start at boot, and check it:</p>
    <pre><code>sudo systemctl daemon-reload
sudo systemctl enable --now wicit-api
systemctl status wicit-api
curl http://127.0.0.1:8080/actuator/health</code></pre>
    <p>To follow the logs, run <code>journalctl -u wicit-api -f</code>. Other commands you may need:</p>
    <table>
        <thead>
        <tr>
            <th>Command</th>
            <th>What it does</th>
        </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td><code>sudo systemctl restart wicit-api</code></td>
            <td>Restarts the API, for example after uploading a new jar.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><code>sudo systemctl stop wicit-api</code></td>
            <td>Stops it until the next start or reboot.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><code>sudo systemctl disable wicit-api</code></td>
            <td>Stops it from starting at boot, but keeps the service.</td>
        </tr>
        </tbody>
    </table>

    <h3 id="remove-the-service">Go back to running it manually</h3>
    <p>To roll back from the service to the manual way, stop the service, turn off its start at boot, and delete it:</p>
    <pre><code>sudo systemctl disable --now wicit-api
sudo rm /etc/systemd/system/wicit-api.service
sudo systemctl daemon-reload
sudo systemctl reset-failed wicit-api 2&gt;/dev/null || true
systemctl status wicit-api</code></pre>
    <p>The last command should say <code>Unit wicit-api.service could not be found</code>. Nothing else changes: the
        jar, <code>.env</code> and the database stay as they are. Start the API as in
        <a href="#run-manually">Option 1</a>. To switch back to the service later, repeat
        <a href="#run-as-a-service">Option 2</a>.</p>

    <h2>10. Serve it over HTTPS with Caddy</h2>
    <p>Caddy has no package for Amazon Linux, so download its single binary and give it a system user:</p>
    <pre><code>sudo curl -fL -o /usr/local/bin/caddy "https://caddyserver.com/api/download?os=linux&amp;arch=amd64"
sudo chmod +x /usr/local/bin/caddy
caddy version
sudo groupadd --system caddy
sudo useradd --system --gid caddy --create-home --home-dir /var/lib/caddy --shell /sbin/nologin caddy
sudo mkdir -p /etc/caddy</code></pre>
    <p>Create <code>/etc/systemd/system/caddy.service</code>. This is Caddy's standard service file. It lets Caddy
        listen on ports 80 and 443 without running as root:</p>
    <pre><code>sudo tee /etc/systemd/system/caddy.service &gt; /dev/null &lt;&lt;'EOF'
[Unit]
Description=Caddy
After=network-online.target
Wants=network-online.target

[Service]
Type=notify
User=caddy
Group=caddy
ExecStart=/usr/local/bin/caddy run --environ --config /etc/caddy/Caddyfile
ExecReload=/usr/local/bin/caddy reload --config /etc/caddy/Caddyfile --force
TimeoutStopSec=5s
LimitNOFILE=1048576
PrivateTmp=true
ProtectSystem=full
AmbientCapabilities=CAP_NET_ADMIN CAP_NET_BIND_SERVICE

[Install]
WantedBy=multi-user.target
EOF</code></pre>
    <p>Create <code>/etc/caddy/Caddyfile</code>:</p>
    <pre><code>sudo tee /etc/caddy/Caddyfile &gt; /dev/null &lt;&lt;'EOF'
{
	# Requests to an IP address carry no server name, so tell Caddy which certificate to use.
	default_sni 44.254.208.38
}

44.254.208.38 {
	# Let's Encrypt only issues IP address certificates with its short-lived profile.
	tls {
		issuer acme {
			profile shortlived
		}
	}
	encode gzip
	reverse_proxy 127.0.0.1:8080
}
EOF</code></pre>
    <p>Check the file, then start Caddy and watch it get the certificate:</p>
    <pre><code>caddy validate --config /etc/caddy/Caddyfile
sudo systemctl daemon-reload
sudo systemctl enable --now caddy
journalctl -u caddy -f</code></pre>
    <p>Then check from your own computer:</p>
    <pre><code>curl https://44.254.208.38/actuator/health</code></pre>
    <p>The API reads the <code>X-Forwarded-*</code> headers Caddy sends, so links it generates, such as in Swagger UI
        at <code>https://44.254.208.38/swagger-ui.html</code>, use HTTPS.</p>
    <p class="note">If Let's Encrypt refuses the IP address certificate, use the host name
        <code>44-254-208-38.sslip.io</code>, which resolves to the same address. Put it in place of both
        <code>44.254.208.38</code> lines in the Caddyfile and remove the global options block and the <code>tls</code>
        block. Caddy then gets an ordinary 90-day certificate. Many people share sslip.io's Let's Encrypt limits, so an
        IP address certificate or <a href="#use-api-findwic-com">api.findwic.com</a> is more reliable.</p>

    <h2 id="point-the-website-at-the-api">11. Point the website at the API</h2>
    <p>The website reads the API's address from <code>VITE_API_BASE_URL</code> when it is built. In the GitHub
        repository that publishes the website, go to Settings → Secrets and variables → Actions → Variables. Set the
        <code>VITE_API_BASE_URL</code> repository variable to <code>https://44.254.208.38</code>, with no trailing
        slash. Then run the <code>Deploy to GitHub Pages</code> workflow again.</p>
    <p>The API already allows requests from <code>https://*.github.io</code>, so no CORS change is needed. To check it
        from your computer:</p>
    <pre><code>curl -sI -H "Origin: https://natebass.github.io" https://44.254.208.38/api/datasets | grep -i access-control-allow-origin</code></pre>

    <h2>Update the server</h2>
    <p>After you change the code, build and upload the jar from your computer as in <a href="#build-and-upload">step
        8</a>, then restart the API. If it runs as a service:</p>
    <pre><code>ssh -i $Key $Server "sudo systemctl restart wicit-api"</code></pre>
    <p>If it runs manually, stop it and start it again. A command sent over <code>ssh</code> doesn't load
        <code>PATH</code> from <code>/etc/profile.d</code>, so it uses Java's full path. The brackets in
        <code>[w]icit</code> stop <code>pkill</code> from matching, and killing, the SSH command itself:</p>
    <pre><code>ssh -i $Key $Server "pkill -f '[w]icit-api-0.0.1-SNAPSHOT.jar'; sleep 3; cd ~/api-wicit-doc/api-wicit-java &amp;&amp; nohup /opt/java/corretto-27/bin/java -Xmx384m -jar build/libs/wicit-api-0.0.1-SNAPSHOT.jar &gt; ~/wicit-api.log 2&gt;&amp;1 &lt; /dev/null &amp;"</code></pre>
    <p>After you change the schema or replace a data file, run <code>.\gradlew.bat update</code> locally, then dump,
        upload and restore the database as in <a href="#load-the-data">step 7</a>. Restart the API afterwards.</p>
    <p>Pull on the server too, so its copy of the repository matches what is running:</p>
    <pre><code>ssh -i $Key $Server "cd ~/api-wicit-doc &amp;&amp; git pull"</code></pre>
    <p class="note">Never run <code>./gradlew</code> on the instance. If it freezes, stop and start it from the EC2
        console. The Elastic IP stays the same.</p>

    <h2>Troubleshooting</h2>
    <table>
        <thead>
        <tr>
            <th>Problem</th>
            <th>What to check</th>
        </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td>The service fails with <code>status=203/EXEC</code></td>
            <td>systemd can't run <code>/opt/java/corretto-27/bin/java</code>. Run
                <code>/opt/java/corretto-27/bin/java -version</code>. If it fails, recreate the link as in
                <a href="#install-jdk">step 3</a>.
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td>The API stops at startup</td>
            <td>For the service, <code>journalctl -u wicit-api -n 100</code>. For a manual run,
                <code>tail -n 100 ~/wicit-api.log</code>. A missing <code>DB_</code> variable or a wrong password shows
                up here.
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td>The instance freezes or the API is killed</td>
            <td>The instance ran out of memory. Stop and start it from the EC2 console, check that swap is on with
                <code>swapon --show</code>, and never run Gradle on it.
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><code>Port 8080 was already in use</code></td>
            <td>The API is already running the other way. Check with <code>pgrep -af wicit-api</code> and
                <code>systemctl status wicit-api</code>, and stop one of them.
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td>Caddy can't get a certificate</td>
            <td><code>journalctl -u caddy -n 100</code>. Check that ports 80 and 443 are open to everyone in the
                security group.
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td>The browser blocks the website's requests</td>
            <td>Open the developer console. A mixed-content error means <code>VITE_API_BASE_URL</code> starts with
                <code>http://</code>. A CORS error means the website's origin isn't allowed in
                <code>application.yaml</code>.
            </td>
        </tr>
        </tbody>
    </table>

    <h2 id="use-api-findwic-com">Use api.findwic.com</h2>
    <p>The <code>findwic.com</code> domain is managed in Route 53 in the organization's WICit AWS account. Point
        <code>api.findwic.com</code> at the Elastic IP to give the API a stable name with an ordinary 90-day
        certificate.</p>

    <h3>Sign in with the SystemAdministrator role</h3>
    <p>Open the organization's AWS access portal, expand the <strong>WICit</strong> account, and choose
        <strong>SystemAdministrator</strong>, not AdministratorAccess. Use the least access the task needs.
        SystemAdministrator includes full Route 53 access (<code>route53:*</code>) and can also edit the EC2 security
        group.</p>

    <h3>Add the DNS record</h3>
    <ol>
        <li>Open <strong>Route 53 → Hosted zones</strong> and choose <code>findwic.com</code>. If the zone isn't
            there, the domain's DNS is hosted somewhere else. Run <code>dig NS findwic.com</code> to see which name
            servers answer for it, and add the record there.
        </li>
        <li>Choose <strong>Create record</strong> and enter:
            <table>
                <thead>
                <tr>
                    <th>Field</th>
                    <th>Value</th>
                </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                <tr>
                    <td>Record name</td>
                    <td><code>api</code></td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td>Record type</td>
                    <td><code>A</code></td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td>Alias</td>
                    <td>Off. An Elastic IP isn't an alias target.</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td>Value</td>
                    <td><code>44.254.208.38</code></td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td>TTL</td>
                    <td><code>300</code></td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td>Routing policy</td>
                    <td>Simple routing</td>
                </tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
        </li>
        <li>Choose <strong>Create records</strong>.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>No other record is needed. Don't add an <code>AAAA</code> record unless the instance has an IPv6 address.</p>

    <h3>Check the CAA records</h3>
    <p>CAA records list which certificate authorities may issue certificates for the domain. Check for them:</p>
    <pre><code>dig +short CAA findwic.com</code></pre>
    <p>If this prints nothing, Let's Encrypt is allowed and there is nothing to do. If it prints records and none of
        them is <code>0 issue "letsencrypt.org"</code>, add that value to the existing <code>CAA</code> record for
        <code>findwic.com</code> in the hosted zone.</p>

    <h3>Check that the name resolves</h3>
    <pre><code>dig +short api.findwic.com</code></pre>
    <p>It should print <code>44.254.208.38</code>, usually within a few minutes.</p>

    <h3>Or add it with the AWS CLI</h3>
    <p>To use the CLI, set up a profile for the same account and role through the access portal, then sign in:</p>
    <pre><code>aws configure sso --profile wicit-sysadmin
aws sso login --profile wicit-sysadmin</code></pre>
    <p>Find the hosted zone ID, then create or update the record:</p>
    <pre><code>aws route53 list-hosted-zones-by-name --dns-name findwic.com --profile wicit-sysadmin \
  --query 'HostedZones[0].Id' --output text

aws route53 change-resource-record-sets --profile wicit-sysadmin \
  --hosted-zone-id ZONE_ID \
  --change-batch '{
    "Comment": "WICit API on EC2",
    "Changes": [{
      "Action": "UPSERT",
      "ResourceRecordSet": {
        "Name": "api.findwic.com",
        "Type": "A",
        "TTL": 300,
        "ResourceRecords": [{"Value": "44.254.208.38"}]
      }
    }]
  }'</code></pre>
    <p>Put the ID from the first command, without the <code>/hostedzone/</code> prefix, in place of
        <code>ZONE_ID</code>.</p>

    <h3>Serve the domain from Caddy</h3>
    <p>Replace the whole of <code>/etc/caddy/Caddyfile</code>. Don't append to it: the global options block, the one
        with no address, must come first and appear only once. Keep the IP address block until the website has
        switched, so both addresses work in the meantime:</p>
    <pre><code>sudo tee /etc/caddy/Caddyfile &gt; /dev/null &lt;&lt;'EOF'
{
	default_sni 44.254.208.38
}

api.findwic.com {
	encode gzip
	reverse_proxy 127.0.0.1:8080
}

44.254.208.38 {
	tls {
		issuer acme {
			profile shortlived
		}
	}
	encode gzip
	reverse_proxy 127.0.0.1:8080
}
EOF</code></pre>
    <p>Check the file, then reload Caddy and check the new name:</p>
    <pre><code>caddy validate --config /etc/caddy/Caddyfile
sudo systemctl reload caddy
curl https://api.findwic.com/actuator/health</code></pre>
    <p>If the reload fails, Caddy keeps running with its old configuration, so the new name gets a TLS error such as
        <code>tlsv1 alert internal error</code>. Read the full error with
        <code>journalctl -u caddy --no-pager -n 30</code>, fix the file, and reload again.</p>
    <p>If Caddy can't get the certificate, check that the DNS record resolves and that ports 80 and 443 are open.</p>

    <h3>Switch the website</h3>
    <p>Set the <code>VITE_API_BASE_URL</code> repository variable to <code>https://api.findwic.com</code> and run the
        <code>Deploy to GitHub Pages</code> workflow again, as in <a href="#point-the-website-at-the-api">step
            11</a>. No CORS change is needed: the API allows the website's origins, and its own host name doesn't
        matter for CORS.</p>
    <p>Once the website uses the new name, you can remove the global options block and the
        <code>44.254.208.38</code> block from the Caddyfile and reload Caddy.</p>
{% endblock %}
